## Method
Every step runs in a single Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, using the `gars-bio`
environment (`$GARS_PY`, via `_system/gars-env.sh`), submitted as one batch job. Inputs are
read only; nothing is written outside this analysis directory.

1. **Resolve inputs.** At run time, call
   `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project <project> --assay rnaseq_bulk --type counts_gene`
   and `--type design`. Abort if either resolution fails (`ok: false`). Do not fall back to the
   paths in the Inputs table.
2. **Load and align.** Read the count matrix (tab-separated; columns `gene_id`, `gene_name`,
   then one column per sample). Index rows by `gene_id` and drop `gene_name`. Read the design
   (`sample_id,condition,group,replicate`). Order the count columns by the design's
   `sample_id`. Abort if any design sample has no column in the matrix, or the matrix has a
   sample column that is not in the design.
3. **Prepare counts.** The salmon length-scaled counts are non-integer, so round them to the
   nearest integer (pydeseq2 requires integer counts). Drop genes whose count is zero in all
   six samples. Log how many genes remain.
4. **Variance-stabilising transform.** Use pydeseq2 0.5.4: build a `DeseqDataSet` with design
   `~condition`, then call `vst(use_design=False)`. This is a blind VST, like DESeq2's
   `vst(blind=TRUE)`, so the transform does not see the treated/untreated labels. Use the
   default parametric dispersion fit and median-of-ratios size factors.
5. **Feature selection.** Keep the 500 genes with the highest variance of VST values across
   samples. This matches the default of DESeq2's `plotPCA` (`ntop = 500`).
6. **PCA.** Run `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` (scikit-learn 1.9.0) on the samples x 500-gene
   matrix. Centre each gene; do not scale to unit variance (also the `plotPCA` convention). Use
   `n_components = 5`, the most components that six samples can have after centring. Record
   per-sample scores and each component's explained-variance ratio.
7. **Plot.** Use matplotlib 3.11.1 to draw a PC1-vs-PC2 scatter, one point per sample. Colour
   by `condition` (treated vs untreated) and label each point with its `sample_id`. Each axis
   label shows the percent variance explained (e.g. `PC1 (xx.x%)`). Save it as PNG at 300 dpi.
8. **Write outputs.** Write the tables and the figure listed under Outputs, plus a log of the
   run parameters, gene counts after each filter and package versions. Print the log to
   stdout as well, so it lands in the batch log.

